<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Túpac Amaru II</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1738">1738</a>–<a href="/years/1781">1781</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a> · Also known as José Gabriel Condorcanqui</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tupac-amaru">Túpac Amaru</a> II, born José Gabriel Condorcanqui, was a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kuraka">kuraka</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cusco">Cusco</a> region who claimed descent from the last <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/inca-empire">Inca</a> ruler, Túpac Amaru. In November 1780 he led a great rebellion against Spanish colonial abuses that spread across the southern Andes. He was captured in 1781 and executed in the main square of Cusco, but he became a symbol of indigenous resistance and Peruvian independence.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/tupac-amaru-ii.jpg" alt="Túpac Amaru II" width="297" height="500" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:TupacAmaruII.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Memory of the Incas inspired later resistance, from the Neo-Inca State at Vilcabamba to the great rebellion of <b>Túpac Amaru II</b> in 1780.</blockquote><a href="/empire/inca-empire">Inca Empire · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>The execution of Túpac Amaru in Cusco’s main square became a lasting symbol of colonial injustice, and in 1780 <b>José Gabriel Condorcanqui</b>, who took the name Túpac Amaru II, led the great Andean revolt against Spanish rule.</blockquote><a href="/empire/neo-inca-state">Neo-Inca State · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>Lima was the seat of Spain’s oldest and richest South American viceroyalty, heir to the core of the Inca Empire, and its creole elite, shaken by the great rebellion of <b>Túpac Amaru II</b> in 1780–1781, stayed mostly loyal to the crown.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-peru">Republic of Peru · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Heavier taxation and the abuses of the reparto fed the great rebellion of <b>Túpac Amaru II</b>, a kuraka who claimed descent from the Inca rulers, in 1780; it spread across the southern Andes and Upper Peru before he was captured and executed at Cuzco in 1781, and tens…</blockquote><a href="/empire/viceroyalty-of-peru">Viceroyalty of Peru · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>After the great rebellion led by <b>Túpac Amaru II</b> in 1780 and 1781, however, the colonial authorities suppressed expressions of Inca identity, and the use of Quechua in writing declined.</blockquote><a href="/language/quechua">Quechua · Speakers &amp; Spread</a></li><li><blockquote>In the 18th century rising royal demands squeezed them between the state and their communities; in 1780 the kuraka <b>Túpac Amaru II</b> led the Andean rebellion.</blockquote><a href="/societies/spanish-america">Social Hierarchy of Spanish America</a></li></ul></section>
